d with the light at your back, and spray a fine mist of water from a spray bottle in front of you, you *can* see a tiny, faint rainbow. It’s just not something you’d ever stumble upon accidentally. So it’s not that other light sources *can’t* do it—it’s that the result is so weak and blurry that it vanishes unless everything is perfectly set up. In short: your observation that streetlights never give rainbows is correct because the brightness, geometry, and viewing conditions are all stacked against it in daily life. The sun is almost the only natural source that blasts enough light in parallel to make a bow that’s both sharp and bright enough for us to notice.
